 - **Amended 2026-10-30: generic and refined subject domains.** Begin coverage normalization with the TypeScript type of the subject expression at its evaluation point, including represented flow refinements, and use its effective base constraint when one exists. Finite constrained parameters are checked conservatively over that constraint; unconstrained or broad parameters stay open. A represented intersection refinement may reduce the domain; a bare type parameter keeps its whole constraint even after a guard. Preserve literal, Boolean, enum and tag-case identity rather than boxing every subject with apparent-type conversion. This changes neither runtime tests nor ordinary arm/default-flow types.
